Expected Observations
Autoencoder
The Autoencoder learns a deterministic compressed representation of each image. It generally produces clear reconstructions because it directly minimizes reconstruction error.
VAE
The VAE learns a probabilistic latent representation. Its KL-divergence term encourages a smooth and structured latent space. Consequently, VAE reconstructions may be smoother than AE reconstructions, but the learned latent space supports generation of new images.
Image Generation
Random vectors sampled from the VAE's regularized latent distribution are passed through the decoder to generate new digit-like images.
AE vs VAE Comparison
Feature	Autoencoder	Variational Autoencoder
Latent representation	Deterministic vector	Probabilistic distribution
Encoder output	Latent vector	Mean and log variance
Reconstruction	Usually sharper	Often smoother
Reconstruction loss	MSE	Reconstruction component
KL divergence	Not used	Used
Structured latent space	Not explicitly enforced	Explicitly encouraged
Random generation	Not guaranteed	Supported
Main purpose	Reconstruction/compression	Reconstruction and generation


